\begin{proof}
Let $p$ be prime. If $p\mid a$, then $a\equiv 0\pmod p$, so $a^p\equiv a\pmod p$. Otherwise, the residue class $[a]$ belongs to the multiplicative group $(\mathbb Z/p\mathbb Z)^\times$, whose order is $p-1$. By Lagrange's Theorem, the order of $[a]$ divides $p-1$, and hence $[a]^{p-1}=[1]$. Multiplying by $[a]$ gives $[a]^p=[a]$, so $a^p\equiv a\pmod p$.
\end{proof}
